#
# Two identical references to a rule whose condition cannot be evaluated.
#
# `Enabled !EMPTY` against a bool is undecidable, so `inner` fails for "the condition could not be
# evaluated" and both references inherit that answer -- a reference cannot know more than the rule did.
# `outer_first` and `outer_second` are character-for-character identical apart from their names, so the
# two must be explained identically. Anything else is the evaluation order showing through.
#
# It does show through, because the rule-status cache memoises the answer per (rule, role). The first
# reference misses the cache and re-evaluates `inner`, which records the type error under the
# referencing rule's own condition subtree, where the reason is read from. The second reference hits the
# cache, records nothing, and had nothing to read: it printed the verdict sentence and stopped, in the
# same run as two rules above it that printed the reason in full.
#
rule inner when Enabled !EMPTY {
    Name == "irrelevant"
}

rule outer_first when inner {
    Name == "definitely_not_this"
}

rule outer_second when inner {
    Name == "definitely_not_this"
}
